theory.intro:
Every probability is a number from $0$ to $1$. Where it sits on this scale says how likely the event is.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\draw (0,0)--(7,0);
\foreach \x in {0,3.5,7} {\draw (\x,-0.12)--(\x,0.12);}
\node[below] at (0,-0.15) {$0$};
\node[below] at (3.5,-0.15) {$\frac{1}{2}$};
\node[below] at (7,-0.15) {$1$};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (0,-1.3) {$0\%$};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (3.5,-1.3) {$50\%$};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (7,-1.3) {$100\%$};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (0,-2.0) {Impossible};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (3.5,-2.0) {Even chance};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (7,-2.0) {Certain};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.75,0.45) {Unlikely};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (5.25,0.45) {Likely};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
Describe each event as impossible, unlikely, even chance, likely or certain.
(a) Rolling a number greater than $2$ on a fair six-sided die.
(b) Rolling a $2$ on the same die.
(c) Tossing a fair coin and getting tails.
(d) Rolling a $7$ on the die.
(e) Rolling a number less than $7$ on the die.

The probability scale below has equally spaced ticks. Match events (a), (b) and (c) to A, B or C.
